WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:07.209
A medical centre wants to use AI. Here's the plan — made in one conversation with Cunning Claw.

2
00:00:07.459 --> 00:00:16.941
Cwm Taf Valley is made up: nine GPs, eighteen thousand patients, and a daily pile of calls, letters and requests.

3
00:00:17.191 --> 00:00:24.662
The practice manager asks for a plan: the Claw on every PC, and one AI server in the building.

4
00:00:24.912 --> 00:00:27.499
He answers in about twenty seconds.

5
00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:35.699
Each role, the hard rules, three phases — and the saving, written out as a sum.

6
00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:47.344
Every desk gets a helper, and every helper has a line it never crosses. It never judges how urgent something is. It never suggests a diagnosis.

7
00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:59.723
The saving: sixty-two staff hours a week — about eighty-six thousand pounds a year. An estimate, not a promise. Every figure is there to check.

8
00:00:59.973 --> 00:01:12.799
Then the rails: no clinical decisions, a person approves everything, and the paperwork first — DPIA, DSPT, clinical safety, Caldicott.

9
00:01:13.049 --> 00:01:19.971
A rollout in three phases — admin first, GPs last — each one gated on its checks.

10
00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:25.817
Next, the board pack. He's asked to make it himself.

11
00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:34.669
He writes the plan, the spreadsheet and a notice for patients — and checks the sums before he saves them.

12
00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:42.453
Replacing a file can't be undone. So he stops, and asks. Yes.

13
00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:51.101
The board plan, as a PDF.

14
00:01:54.310 --> 00:02:00.423
The savings as a spreadsheet, each assumption in its own column, so the board can change them.

15
00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:09.718
And a one-page notice for patients: a person always decides. It's a draft — it goes up once the sign-offs are real.

16
00:02:09.968 --> 00:02:16.969
Some of it the Claw makes himself. The app, he hands to Claude Code — and asks you first.

17
00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:32.932
Here's how it fits together. Every PC runs the Claw. The AI runs on a server in the building. Patient data stays inside — and anything that would leave waits for a person.

18
00:02:33.182 --> 00:02:38.825
Now the app: a Front Desk prototype for reception, with made-up requests.

19
00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:47.711
The card shows the brief, word for word, and what Claude Code may and may not do. Yes.

20
00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:56.411
Claude Code builds it on its own branch, in under a minute. It never merges. You review it.

21
00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:00.714
The Claw reports back, and opens the preview.

22
00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:09.271
Every request flows the same way. The AI suggests. A person decides.

23
00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:23.579
A receptionist accepts one suggestion, and changes another. Nothing happens until someone clicks — and every decision goes in the log.

24
00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:36.829
Local-first. Asks first. Cunning Claw — free and open source, on your own computer.
